{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And they shall say to me, What is His name? What shall I say to them?</b> It would have been fitting for him to say 'what sign,' for a man who prophesies to a nation in the matter of HaShem is not held to be a prophet, nor do they believe his words, until he gives them a sign for the future; and when the sign and the wonder come to pass as he spoke — all the more so will they hearken to him to follow him. And what Moses said, 'And they shall say to me, What is His name' — it is known that mentioning one of the holy names of the Holy One, blessed be He, is no sign for Israel at all. For if Israel knew that name, then Moses too could presumably know it like them, and what proof would there be in his telling it to them? And if they did not know it and had never heard it, what proof and wonder would there be in the mention of the name, that one who did not believe in Him at first would now believe in Him? And behold, we see that Moses, after the Holy One, blessed be He, made known that name, said, 'And they will not believe me' (Exodus 4:1), and the Holy One, blessed be He, needed to give him signs." "<b>And by way of</b> the plain sense (peshat): 'What is His name' means 'what is the truth of His reality,' and he meant to say that Israel would ask him about the reality of the Name and about the matter of providence, which is the core of faith. Therefore he asked Him to tell him a name that would indicate a complete demonstration concerning reality and providence, for the wise among them, so that they would believe his words and hearken to his voice. Therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, answered him: אהיה אשר אהיה — He made known to him this name, meaning 'the Existent who is the Existent,' that is, the Worthy of existence, and He informed him to explain it to them." "<b>And the Ramban,</b> of blessed memory, wrote in explanation of this name, 'the Existent who is the Existent,' that is, the Worthy of existence, and He informed him to explain it to them — that He is existent, worthy of existence, who was never absent and will never be absent. And according to this matter, that the question of Moses was concerning reality and providence, one must wonder here: how could the elders of Israel have been in doubt about the reality of HaShem, blessed be He, and His providence, when they were sages among sages and recipients of the tradition of the fathers? How would they need to ask for a demonstration from HaShem to clarify for them the matters of reality and providence? And behold, the name would be no proof at all for them, for if there were among them heretics who denied reality, or believers in the eternity of the world, this name would be no proof whatsoever to remove from them that mistaken opinion." "<b>And</b> the sage, the author of Ḥovot haLevavot, wrote: 'And they shall say to me, What is His name' — its meaning is 'what is His essence.' For that which we describe Him — 'Elohim of the heavens' and 'Elohim of the earth,' and likewise 'Elohim of the spirits' — and associate Him with what is other than Him, is because we do not grasp His essence. But had we grasped His essence, we would not need to describe Him and associate Him with what is other than Him. Therefore Moses asked what he would answer if they asked him what His essence is. And because the Name is known from two sides — from the side of the intellect and from the side of received tradition — therefore He answered him and made known to him this name, which is the intellectual side, through which a person grasps Him through His deeds; and since the people would not understand the intellectual side, therefore He mentioned to him also the received tradition, which is what He said: 'HaShem, the Elohim of your fathers, the Elohim of Abraham, the Elohim of Isaac, and the Elohim of Jacob, has sent me to you' (Exodus 3:15) — for this knowledge is from the side of the tradition of the fathers, as it is written (Job 15:18): אשר חכמים יגידו." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: 'And they shall say to me, What is His name' — he sought to know His great name. The Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: 'My name — you seek to know? After My deeds I am called. When I judge the creatures, I am called Elohim. When I wage war against the wicked, I am called Tzevaot. When I suspend a man's sin, I am called Shaddai. When I sit upon the throne of mercy, I am called HaShem (י־י).' The intent of the sages in this midrash is that there is no plurality in unity. And the fact that His names, blessed be He, are many — the intellect knows that they all refer to one Creator, but the mouth cannot encompass them all at once in a single word as the intellect encompasses them. Therefore His names, may He be exalted, are many, multiplying and changing according to the actions, from the side of the recipients, not from His side, blessed be He." "<b>And you should know</b> that here He mentioned the name אהיה three times in a single verse, corresponding to three instances of the [tetragrammaton's root] הויות, to hint that He, blessed be He, rules over three times — past, present, and future — and the name אהיה alone embraces all three of these times: for the word אהיה includes the present time, and its meaning is as though it said 'I am' (אני הוה); and it includes as well the future, and includes as well the past. And the meaning of אהיה אשר אהיה is: I am He who was already, and so too in the special Name [יהוה] are included three הויות, and this is well-known and publicized, and there is no need to elaborate upon it. If so, both of them [the two names] indicate one matter." "<b>And by way of reason (sekhel),</b> in the science of letter-combination: the name אהיה alone embraces these three הויות, for the אל״ף is the beginning of the units, corresponding to 'was' (היה); the ה״א is the middle of the units, corresponding to 'is' (הוה); and the יו״ד is the end of the units and the culmination of all counting, corresponding to 'will be' (יהיה); and the final ה״א is to indicate that He is in all three of these times and His existence in all of them is in the present (הוה) — and the special Name [יהוה] likewise indicates this. And the letter יו״ד was placed at the head because the אל״ף is the beginning of the units and the ט׳ is the end of the units, and when we count nine and wish to count ten, we must each time return to the אל״ף, and it is impossible to complete the count of ten except with the אל״ף; if so, the אל״ף is beginning and end — so too the Creator is beginning and end to all existing beings. And these two letters, the אל״ף and the ט״ית, were absorbed, and from them was made a יו״ד, and it is at the head of the Name, in order to seal the opening and to warn against the impossible question, which is the question of 'what is before' and 'what is after' — for the question of 'what is before' is the אל״ף and the question of 'what is after' is the ט״ית, which is the end; therefore in place of both of them was set the letter יו״ד, which embraces beginning and end, 'was' and 'will be' — the אל״ף corresponding to 'was' and the ט״ית corresponding to 'will be' — to indicate that He, blessed be He, is the beginning of all and the end of all: before Him no El was formed, and after Him none shall be. And then there remain in this name three letters to complete it." "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah:</b> ״And they shall say to me, what is His name?״ — which of His holy names has sent you, that is, by which of His attributes (מידות) are you sent." "<b>And the Ramban, of blessed memory, wrote,</b> and this is his language: 'In my opinion the elders of Israel would not have been in doubt about the existence of the Creator, as the Master [Ibn Ezra] said — far be it and forbidden. Rather, Moses our master, peace be upon him, was at that time also a father in wisdom, great in the rank of prophecy, and he sought a way of asking who the Sender is — that is, by which attribute (מדה) he is sent to them. This is ״And they shall say to me, what is His name?״ — that is, they will ask me about my mission: whether it is in the attribute of El Shaddai, which stood to the fathers, by which hidden miracles were performed for them, or in the attribute of mercy, by which famous miracles, novel in creation, that change the nature of the world, are performed. And therefore he asked, ״What shall I say to them?״ And YHWH, blessed be He, answered him: <b>אהיה אשר אהיה</b> — He informed him that He is sending him to them in the attribute of judgment, which is אשר אהיה, in the attribute of mercy. And He said, <b>״Thus shall you say to the children of Israel: אהיה has sent me to you,״</b> and He did not command him to say 'אהיה אשר אהיה has sent me to you' — the intention being that he should mention to them the one name alone, to indicate unity. And therefore He further commanded him: ״Thus shall you say to the children of Israel: YHWH, etc., has sent me to you״ — for this name is the attribute of mercy, and they will know that He will lead at the right hand of Moses, the arm of His splendor, and will renew signs and wonders in the world. And behold, He explained to him that the אהיה which He commanded him to say to them is this great Name [יהוה], and they are equivalent in their expression and in their letters, for the two final letters of the first name are the preceding ones in this one: for in the first they indicate the wisdom of Solomon, as it is said (I Kings 5:26): ״וה׳ נתן חכמה לשלמה,״ and in this one they indicate the wisdom of Elohim; and the letter אל״ף in the first alludes to precedence and to unity, and the יו״ד in the second to the ten sefirot without 'what' (מה). And the two letters, the אל״ף and the יו״ד, were taken from the two holy names and written at the beginning and the end in the name [Adonai] written and called with אל״ף-דל״ת, to indicate that He is His emanation from head to end, and the attribute of judgment is in the middle, as it says ״כי שמי בקרבו״ — and from this you will understand why it is abbreviated.' Up to here is the language of the Master, of blessed memory.\n\nThe explanation of his words in the expression 'equivalent' (שוים): the אל״ף in this one [אהיה] is the יו״ד in that one [יהוה], for the יו״ד corresponds to the אל״ף, and the ה״א corresponds to the ה״א. There remains יו״ד-ה״א at the end [of אהיה], which alludes to the wisdom of Solomon, corresponding to the וא״ו-ה״א in that one [יהוה], which are likewise an allusion to the wisdom of Solomon. If so, all is one.\n\nI further add an explanation of the Master's words: the name אהי״ה and the name יהו״ה are all one, one name and one matter, for the name אהיה has the same numerical value as יה״ו, which is the core of the special Name, since the ה״א is doubled [appears twice in each].\n\nI further say that we have in our hands a tradition that Moses our master, peace be upon him, sought to know the essence (מהות) of the Name, so that he would be able to give them a clear answer — and this is what he said, ״What is His name?״ — that is, the essence of His name. And from this the special Name was hinted at and inscribed in the language of his question: לי מה שמו — the מ״ה [of the question itself alludes to the Name]. And therefore the matter was necessary to answer him and say: אהיה אשר אהיה, which is the essence — for א״ה alludes to the supreme Crown (כתר עליון), י״ה alludes to Wisdom (חכמה); behold, two sefirot are included in the name אהיה. And furthermore, the name אהיה alludes to Repentance (תשובה), and it is called by the name of the two sefirot preceding it, because it is emanated from their power. And behold, the first אהיה in the verse is Repentance, and the second is upon the two sefirot preceding it, and the meaning of אהיה אשר אהיה is: the one sustained by the first אהיה — and all is one matter. And this name is the highest of all the attributes (מדות) of the Holy One, blessed be He. And to this David, peace be upon him, alluded, saying (Psalms 138:2): ״כי הגדלת על כל שמך אמרתך״ — that is, for You have magnified Your utterance above every name You have; and this is the utterance that He spoke to Moses, as it is written: ״ויאמר אלהים אל משה אהיה אשר אהיה.״ And since Israel were not worthy to have revealed to them what is included in these two names, He revealed it to Moses and commanded him to inform Israel: ״אהיה שלחני אליכם״ — He did not reveal to them in these names anything but the power emanated to them, which is the inwardness of mercy, and He called it אהיה. And He revealed it to the unique ones of the generation — those who would deliver themselves for the public and who would endure the blows in the bondage of mortar and bricks, in order to lighten the yoke of their brethren. And these had already merited a great elevation at the giving of the Torah, as it is written of them (Exodus 24:10): ״ויראו את אלהי ישראל ותחת רגליו כמעשה לבנת הספיר,״ and they are the very ones who now merited knowledge of the name אהיה. And this is why He said ״לבני ישראל אהיה שלחני אליכם״ — in the language of לבנים [to the brick-makers], and He did not say אל בני [to the children of]. And afterward He returned and said: ״כה תאמר אל בני ישראל ה׳ אלהי אבותיכם״ — these [the rest of Israel] were not so great in rank, and He commanded that they be informed by the explicit Name — revelation of the plainly expounded matter, in which there is no such concealment of secret. And afterward He admitted the elders into the secret of עבור (transition/pregnancy), which is what He said: ״פקד פקדתי אתכם,״ in the language of (Exodus 20:5) ״פוקד עון אבות על בנים.״ From this you learn that the Holy One, blessed be He, divided His words into four parts: the first to Moses alone; the second to those who had the merit of the bricks; the third to the rest of Israel; the fourth to the elders of Israel — and He mentioned them last, for He entrusted them with the secret of the Shekhinah, which is the last of the sefirot.\n\nAnd you must know that אהיה, which embraces the first two [sefirot], plus another אהיה which alludes to Repentance (תשובה), plus the third אהיה without the ה״א, which alludes to the inwardness of mercy — which is Tiferet — all three of them are included in the explicit Name [יהוה]: for the יו״ד alludes to Wisdom (חכמה) with its crown (קוץ), which alludes to the Crown (כתר); the [first] ה״א to Repentance (תשובה, = Binah); and the וא״ו to Tiferet; the [final] ה״ה corresponding to the [first] ה״א. From here you will understand that in the special Name, the two first letters are an allusion to His truth (אמתתו), and the two last letters are an allusion to His attributes (מדות). And further, observe that in the Torah the two first letters [of יהוה] stand as a name in their own right, but not so the two last letters, for they are not a name; and the reason for this is that when we mention the two first, the last are included within them, for they are the beginning, and it is impossible for there to be any truncation in them — but the last ones, since they are not a name, had they been a name there would have been in this a truncation; and therefore the two last were not written as a name in their own right. And may the Place show us wonders from His Torah."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {8 "In comment 1, the text discusses the letter ט (tet) as the end of the units (9), paired with א (alef) as the beginning (1), their combination yielding י (yod = 10); rendered hyper-literally as written. In comment 3, the Ramban's comparison of אהיה with יהוה via Adonai (אדנ\"י) is translated verbatim without unpacking the letter-substitution logic; 'קמצתו' (He abbreviated it) preserved as written. The allusion to מ״ה in לי מה שמו is left exactly as the text states."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:56:45.564800564Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}